12846478,2025-04-07,Credit reporting or other personal consumer reports,Credit reporting,Incorrect information on your report,Information belongs to someone else,"Date : XX/XX/XXXX To : Consumer Financial Protection Bureau ( Submitted via online complaint portal ) Re : Complaint Against Experian Failure to Remove Fraudulent Accounts and Inquiries ( FCRA 605B & 611 Violations, FACTA rights, CFPB Enforcement Action XX/XX/XXXX ) Complainant : XXXX XXXX, Victim of Identity Theft ( Address : XXXX XXXX XXXX XXXX, XXXX XXXX, NV XXXX ; DOB : XX/XX/XXXXXXXX  ; SSN : XXXX XXXX XXXX ) Company Complained About : Experian Information Solutions , Inc. ( Experian Credit Bureau ) Issue : Experian has failed to block or delete fraudulent information on my credit report despite my submitting an identity theft report and repeated disputes. This violates my rights under the Fair Credit Reporting Act ( FCRA ), specifically 605B ( 15 U.S.C. 1681c2 ) and 611 ( 15 U.S.C. 1681i ), as amended by the FACT Act of XXXX. It also echoes the pattern of misconduct outlined in the CFPBs XX/XX/XXXX enforcement action against Experian. 

Background : I am an identity theft victim. In XX/XX/XXXX, I discovered multiple accounts on my Experian credit file that I did not open. I promptly filed an FTC Identity Theft Report ( Report # XXXX ) and added a fraud alert to my credit file in XX/XX/XXXX. I then disputed the fraudulent accounts and inquiries with Experian, providing a copy of my FTC Identity Theft Report ( and later a police report, if required ). Under FCRA 605B, once Experian had my identity theft report and identification, they were obligated to block the fraudulent accounts from my file within 4 business days. Additionally, under FCRA 611, Experian was required to conduct a reasonable reinvestigation of my disputes and remove any information that is inaccurate or can not be verified. 

Despite these clear legal requirements, Experian failed to remove the fraudulent items. In XX/XX/XXXX, Experian processed some disputes ( the only change I saw was an updated notation on one account ), but the fraudulent accounts remained. I have since obtained my updated Experian report on XX/XX/XXXX, and the same fraudulent accounts and even more hard inquiries are still being reported. 

Fraudulent Accounts Experian Refuses to Remove : My Experian report still shows four ( 4 ) accounts that do not belong to me a charged-off XXXX XXXX XXXXXXXX credit card , a XXXX XXXXXXXX account where I was falsely listed as an authorized user, a fraudulent communications account, and a XXXX XXXX XXXX account. These are described below : XXXXXXXX XXXX XXXX XXXX XXXX *opened XX/XX/XXXXXXXX  * {$230.00} balance XXXX XXXX XXXXXXXX Account ending in XXXX ; opened XX/XX/XXXXXXXX  ; {$920.00} charged off. ( I never had a XXXX  card this was identity theft. ) XXXX XXXX Account ending in XXXX ; opened XX/XX/XXXX ; I am reported as an authorized user. ( I have no relationship with this account identity theft. ) XXXX XXXX Account ending in XXXX ; opened XX/XX/XXXX ; {$1200.00} in collections. ( I never had a XXXX account identity theft. ) All of the above accounts were opened by an imposter, not by me. I have provided Experian with an identity theft affidavit and ample proof ( including correspondence from the creditors and my FTC report ), yet Experian continues to report these accounts as if they are valid. This is causing ongoing harm to me my credit score is severely damaged, and I am being wrongfully pursued for debts that arent mine. 

Unauthorized Inquiries : Likewise, I disputed 17 hard inquiries resulting from the fraudulent applications ( auto loans, credit cards, etc. that the thief attempted ). Experian has not removed these inquiries either, even though they were not authorized by me ( and thus lack any permissible purpose under FCRA ). These inquiries ( all from XXXX ) include multiple auto finance companies and banks ( e.g., XXXX XXXX on XX/XX/XXXX, XXXX on XX/XX/XXXX, several credit unions on XX/XX/XXXX, etc. I can provide a full list ). Under FCRA, these inquiries are also information that resulted from identity theft, so they should be blocked just like accounts, or at minimum treated as disputed inaccurate information. Experians failure to address them violates the law. 

