13091149,2025-04-21,Credit reporting or other personal consumer reports,Credit reporting,Problem with a company's investigation into an existing problem,Their investigation did not fix an error on your report,"Subject : Debt Validation and Cease and Desist Request Pursuant to the FDCPA and FCRA Disputed Accounts : To Whom It May Concern, This letter is a formal dispute and request for debt validation regarding the accounts listed above, which appear on my credit report. I do not recognize or acknowledge these accounts as valid, and I am requesting complete validation under the Fair Debt Collection Practices Act ( FDCPA ) and Fair Credit Reporting Act ( FCRA ). 
Please provide the following for each of the accounts listed : 1. The name and address of the original creditor, 2. The itemized amount of the alleged debt, including principal, interest, fees, and other charges, 3. A copy of the original signed contract or agreement allegedly binding me to this debt, 4. The full chain of title including purchase or assignment agreements showing legal transfer of the debt to the current owner, 5. A copy of any correspondence or statements that have been sent to me regarding these accounts. 
Until you provide the documentation above, I am requesting, under 15 U.S. Code 1692c ( c ), that you cease and desist all collection activity, including : Reporting this alleged debt to any credit bureaus Communicating with me directly Selling or transferring the debt Pursuing any legal action Furthermore, in light of the Consumer Financial Protection Bureaus ( CFPB ) enforcement action against Experian ( Case No. XXXX ), in which Experian was found to have engaged in deceptive practices regarding credit reporting and scoring, I am especially concerned about the integrity and accuracy of these disputed items. 
If validation is not provided, I expect all references to these accounts to be immediately removed from my Experian credit file in accordance with FCRA Section 611 ( 15 U.S.C. 1681i ). 
If you continue to report unverifiable information or fail to respond within the required timeframe, I will file formal complaints with the CFPB, FTC, and my states Attorney General, and may seek legal remedies for violations of federal consumer protection laws. 
